<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Messene</h1><p class="st-meta">369 BCE – 146 BCE · capital Messene</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">City founded by Epaminondas in 369 BCE to free the Messenians from centuries of Spartan helotry, with some of the finest surviving Greek fortifications.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>For centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messenians">Messenians</a> had been helots, forced to work their land for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a>. After defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a>, the Theban general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> and in 369 BCE founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zancle">Messene</a> on the slopes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ithome">Mount Ithome</a>, calling back Messenians scattered across the Greek world. Sparta refused for decades to recognise the new state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zancle">Messene</a> was built from scratch with a ring of stone walls some 9 kilometres long, towers and the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadian-gate">Arcadian Gate</a>. It had a council and assembly and celebrated its restored identity in cults of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> Ithomatas and the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristomenes">Aristomenes</a>. Fearing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a>, it sought protection from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a> and later the Hellenistic kings.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zancle">Messene</a> joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League</a> in 191 BCE, revolted, and in 182 BCE executed the captured Achaean general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philopoemen">Philopoemen</a> before being forced back. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaean-war">Achaean War</a> in 146 BCE it passed under Roman rule. Excavations have uncovered its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, stadium and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a>, among the best-preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/messene.jpg" alt="Messene" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Rossignol Benoît · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Messeneasklepeion.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>369 BCE – 146 BCE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Messene</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek (Doric)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ithome, Messenia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-258&amp;focus=messene" aria-label="Messene on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5229 839 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Messene lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5549 1095L5562 1098M5549 1095L5549 1060"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5562 1098h0M5549 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5549 1095h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5549 1095h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-258&amp;focus=messene">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
